<div class="hero-lead"><p class="hero-kicker">When private information surfaces</p><h1>Someone seems to know something private. What now?</h1><p>Someone quotes a private conversation, knows about a plan you have not shared with them, or hints at information they could use against you. You may not know what they actually have or how it reached them.</p><p><a class="btn" href="#contact">Request a private conversation</a></p><p class="trust">In person &#xB7; With you present &#xB7; You control your credentials</p><p>Dr. Lior Tabansky works with you, in person, to examine relevant access and make agreed changes. You can take useful steps without first proving how information became known.</p></div></div></section><nav class="page-index wrap" aria-label="On this page" data-presentation-ui><a href="#start-with-what-can-be-checked">Your situation</a><a href="#have-someone-examine-the-access-and-address-urgent-problems">First paid visit</a><a href="#contact">Contact</a></nav>
<section class="copy-section" id="start-with-what-can-be-checked" aria-labelledby="start-with-what-can-be-checked-heading"><div class="wrap copy-body"><div class="section-title"><h2 id="start-with-what-can-be-checked-heading">Start with what can be checked</h2></div><div class="section-detail">
<p>Relevant exposure may involve several connected devices, identities or services, rather than the account you first suspect. Dr. Tabansky examines those relationships and explains what they can reveal or allow. Location settings, public information and online trails may also matter within the agreed review of your own environment.</p>
<p>The findings can identify access worth changing now, even when they cannot explain the original concern.</p>
</div></div></section>
<section class="copy-section" id="understand-the-effects-before-making-isolated-changes" aria-labelledby="understand-the-effects-before-making-isolated-changes-heading"><div class="wrap copy-body"><div class="section-title"><h2 id="understand-the-effects-before-making-isolated-changes-heading">Understand the effects before making isolated changes</h2></div><div class="section-detail">
<p>Changing a password may leave another session or recovery route open. Deleting an account can also affect services you still need. We explain those connections and agree the sequence of changes with you.</p>
<p>Some changes can be noticed, such as a signed-in device being disconnected. We do not promise invisibility. The aim is informed action within the access you are authorized to manage.</p>
</div></div></section>

